    "lawCode": "Amd §92-f, Pub Serv L",
    "lawSection": "Public Service Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S2356A\n\nSPONSOR: PARKER\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the public service law, in relation to the expiration of\nminutes purchased with prepaid cellular telephone cards\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL:\n\nTo conspicuously disclose expiration dates on prepaid cellular calling\ncards.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nAdds section 92-g to the Public Service Law to require disclosure of the\nexpiration date of the cellular minutes on a prepaid cellular calling\ncard.\n\n \nDIFFERENCE BETWEEN ORIGINAL AND AMENDED VERSION (IF APPLICABLE):\n\nSpecific large print requirements.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nAlthough current law requires disclosure of the expiration date, such\ninformation is often in very small print located on the back of a card\nhenceforth ascertaining the expiration date is often difficult.The popu-\nlarity of no contract prepaid cellular service is increasing and consum-\ners have a right to know the terms of use without having to search\nthrough a myriad of disclosures and small fonts. Consumers often use the\nexpiration date as the critical factor in selecting a prepaid card and\nas such it should be prominently displayed.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\n2015-2016: S2561/A7667-Referred to Energy & Telecommunications\n2013-2014: S.2771-Referred to Energy & Telecommunications\n2011-2012:  S.1085-Referred to Energy & Telecommunications\n2009-2010:  S.7271-Referred to Energy & Telecommunications\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect on the one hundred eightieth day after it\nshall have become a law.\n",
